 "overview": {
  "summary": "A surface-mount daughter card on the Oscillator Output assembly (A13), 'U30' on A13's silkscreen, that is the gain block of the 22 V output amplifier. It is an operational amplifier built from discrete parts to get the speed, output power and breakdown voltage a 22 V rms output at 1.2 MHz needs. The input stage is a differential pair Q2/Q3 buffered by source follower Q1, with its transconductance set by R3, R4 and R26; its inherent offset is corrected by U1 and U2, with R30 trimming the dc zero at U2's output, and Q3's output is level-shifted through VR5-VR7 into the mid stage. Q4 is a high-impedance current sink biasing the input stage at about 10 mA. The mid stage is a common-emitter Miller-compensated gain stage, Q5, biased to 10 mA by Q6, current-limited by R12 and Q4, with the dominant pole set by C5; it drives common-base level shifter Q13 on A13. The only parts of the output stage on the card are its input transistors Q7 and Q8, biased by R17 and CR4/CR5 to about 40 mA, which drive A13's bootstrapped complementary Darlington output stage.",
  "sections": "§2-136, §2-137",
  "note": "The card runs from A13's +155 V and -125 V rails and its output driver lines carry the full output swing, so it is live at high voltage whenever A13 is in operate. Nothing in Chapter 5 addresses it directly: §5-18 step 8 puts A13's S1 in the test position, checks 1 V across A13's R87 (the card's 10 mA current source) and +3.2 V at A13's TP2, and decides between this card and A13's output stage from those two readings."
 },
